-- title:
--   Integrality bounds from regularity of x djmath̄ on X₀(N)_ℚ̄
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ and write $\overline F_N$ for [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111), the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\overline{\mathbb Q}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the subfield $\mathbb Q\bigl(\text{divisorExpansions } N\bigr) \subseteq \mathbb Q((q))$ under the coefficient embedding induced by $\mathbb Q \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$. Let $\bar\jmath \in \overline F_N$ be the element given by the image of the Laurent series [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) $= q^{-1}\cdot(\text{the rational }j\text{-numerator power series})$ under that embedding, which lies in $\overline F_N$ because `jq` lies in `modularFunctionField N`, itself contained in `modularFunctionFieldFull N`. Let $x \in \overline F_N$ and assume that the Kähler differential $x \cdot d\bar\jmath \in \Omega_{\overline F_N / \overline{\mathbb Q}}$ lies in `regularDifferentialsBar N`, i.e. for every place $v$ of $\overline F_N$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ there is $f$ in the valuation subring of $v$ with $x \cdot d\bar\jmath = f \cdot v.\mathrm{dCoord}$. The conclusion is a conjunction: first, $x^{6}\,\bar\jmath^{\,4}\,(\bar\jmath - 1728)^{3}$ is integral over the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-subalgebra generated by $\bar\jmath$; second, with $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$ denoting `dedekindPsi N`, the element $x^{2\psi(N)}\,\bar\jmath^{\,\psi(N)+1}\,(\bar\jmath - 1728)^{\psi(N)}$ is integral over the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-subalgebra generated by $\bar\jmath^{-1}$. Here $1728$ is the image of $1728 \in \overline{\mathbb Q}$ in $\overline F_N$.
--
--   This is the algebraic half of the identification of regular differentials on $X_0(N)$ with weight-two cusp forms: regularity of $\omega = x\,d\bar\jmath$ at every place is converted into two integrality relations, one over $\overline{\mathbb Q}[\bar\jmath]$ (controlling the ramification of $\bar\jmath$ at its zeros, at $\bar\jmath = 1728$ and elsewhere) and one over $\overline{\mathbb Q}[\bar\jmath^{-1}]$ (controlling the poles of $\bar\jmath$, of total degree $\psi(N)$). It is used in the passage from a regular differential to a cusp form with the corresponding $q$-expansion, and in the comparison of regular differentials over a residue field.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_isIntegral_and_isIntegral_of_smul_D_mem_regularDifferentialsBar.lean

